1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:06,900
line is the one that most beginners skip. So I would say do not skip it. Missing data in a column,

2
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:13,300
you use for filtering will silently break your analysis. Rows just disappear without warning.

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:19,060
I learned this lesson the hard way on a reporting data set where I was comparing performance

4
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:25,700
month over month. Everything looked fine until I noticed one region has apparently collapsed overnight.

5
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:31,780
The problem wasn't the business. It was missing values. The date field for a subset of records

6
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:37,540
had failed to parse correctly, turning into nulls. When I filtered for a date range,

7
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:44,900
pandas quietly excluded these rows. And the analysis suggested a dramatic revenue drop that never

8
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:50,820
actually happened. The numbers were technically correct for the filter data, but the filter data

9
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:57,060
itself was wrong. Since then, is null.sum is one of the first commands I run on any data set.

10
00:00:57,700 --> 00:00:57,700
What we need to do here is, so now we are going to focus on selecting the right rows and columns.
